Example 1: 
	We may write the vector (1, -2) as i - 2j and the vector (7, π, -3) as 7i + πj -3k. 


Example 2: 
	The set of equations 
		
		x = 2cos(t) 
				0 <= t < 2π 
		y = 2sin(t)
	
	describes a circle of radius 2, since we may check that 

		x² + y² = (2cos(t))² + (2sin(t))² = 4. 

	Parametric equations may be used as readily to discribe curves in R³; a curve in R³ is the set of points (x,y,z) whose coordinates x, y, and z are each given by a function of t: 

			x = f(t)
			y = g(t)
			z = h(t)

The advantages of using parametric equations are twofold. 
	
		1) They offer a unifor way o discribe curves in any number of deminsions
		2) They allow you to get a dynamic sense of a curve if you consider the parameter variable t to represent time and imagine that a particle is traveling along the curve with time according to the given parametric equations. 

